  { q: 'Does the Rs 50 lakh threshold apply when buying from an NRI?', a: 'No. The Rs 50 lakh threshold and the 1 percent rate are for a resident seller. When the seller is a non-resident, the buyer deducts under section 393(2) at the rates applicable to the income, whatever the value of the property. Do not apply the 1 percent rule to an NRI seller.' },
  { q: 'Can the buyer deduct on the capital gain instead of the full consideration?', a: 'Only if the seller gives a lower or nil deduction certificate from the Assessing Officer, or the buyer can reliably establish the gain. The buyer usually does not know the seller\'s cost, so without a certificate the deduction is commonly made on the full sale consideration at the rate for the gain.' },
  { q: 'Does an individual buyer need a TAN for property bought from an NRI?', a: 'Not for transactions from 1 October 2026. The Department\'s help page for Form 141 Schedule E says a TAN is no longer mandatory when a resident individual or HUF buys from a non-resident, and the buyer\'s PAN is used instead. Other buyers should check the requirement on the portal.' },
  { q: 'Who files Form 145 and Form 146 when the NRI remits the money abroad?', a: 'The NRI seller files the remittance declaration, Form 145 (earlier Form 15CA), through the portal or the bank. Form 146 (earlier Form 15CB) is a Chartered Accountant\'s certificate, needed where the remittance is taxable and above the limit set in the rules. The buyer\'s role is deducting and reporting TDS.' },
];
